From: DKR-R Windows 95 port
Subject: [PATCH] A precise clock and a punctual VI thread

Everything ultramodern times went through std::chrono::high_resolution_clock:
osGetCount, osGetTime, the game's timers and the VI thread's schedule. With
mingw-w64 that is the system clock, over winpthreads' CLOCK_REALTIME, and on
Windows 95 it falls back to GetSystemTimeAsFileTime. Measured on the target: 4
steps in 200 ms, the largest 60 ms. ultramodern::precise_now() keeps the time
point type but advances with steady_clock, which winpthreads takes from
QueryPerformanceCounter: 18,325 steps in 200 ms, the largest 77 us.

The VI loop sent a retrace on every pass. Early wakes on the coarse clock gave
the game 66.5 retraces a second instead of 60, and 109 with a profiler forcing
the scheduler every millisecond. It now sends nothing until the retrace count
has moved.

On Windows 95, set_native_thread_priority applies Critical (the VI thread) and
VeryHigh (the timer thread). A quarter of the VI thread's wakes had come 16 ms
or more late, waiting out whoever held the processor. They are now all under
2 ms, with a mean of 1.3 ms. Every other thread keeps normal priority.

Normal mode with both opt-in options: 40.3 ms -> 37.3 ms (26.8 fps). 2,160
display lists, no rejects, clean exit. The instrument, [trace][vi-late], is
part of this patch.

